Subject: Fixes the deferred-RC exact-count operations and drains the zero-count table at loop back edges

What this does
  Fixes the deferred-RC exact-count operations and drains the zero-count table at loop back edges.

Why we need it
  Under deferred RC a value can be held by a register without that register owning it, so `JS_REF_COUNT(x) == 1` no longer proves unique ownership and the in-place string append / closure-cycle break would mutate or free a value another register still sees; separately.

What it adds
  `JS_DRC_UNIQUELY_OWNED(p)` gates `JS_ConcatString2`'s in-place append, `js_closcyc_frame` skips the self-cycle break under `QJS_DRC` (the collector sees the closure -> var_ref edge); `REGVM_DRC_BACKEDGE` in `T_NEXT`/`T_BR_NEXT` tests `off < 0` and the table bound inline and tail-calls the new `regvm_t_safepoint_slow` to reconcile and re-enter the chain at the branch target.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
--- a/quickjs.c
